To guarantee your Information Security Department (ISD) finds zero XSS or HTML injection vulnerabilities, the most foolproof enterprise approach is to strictly block all angle brackets (< and >) alongside script protocols. If the system only expects plain text, rejecting < and > entirely eliminates the possibility of executing <script>, <iframe>, <img>, or any other malicious tag.
Here is exactly what to replace in your code to implement this strict security gate.
1. Add this method to the bottom of your class
Add this validation helper inside your service class. This acts as an aggressive security filter.
private void validateSecureInput(String input, String fieldName) {
    if (input == null || input.isBlank()) return;
    
    // Strictly blocks ANY angle brackets (< >), preventing all HTML/XML tag injections, 
    // as well as script pseudo-protocols.
    if (input.matches(".*[<>].*") || input.matches(".*(?i)(javascript|vbscript|data):.*")) {
        throw new BadRequestException(
            "Security validation failed for " + fieldName + ": Input contains restricted characters or markup. Please use plain text and resubmit."
        );
    }
}

